Open the web UI on an address, by writing /etc/open-linux-router/olrd.env and
restarting the service.

olr always serves its control socket, which is what the command line
talks to. It listens on the network only when told to, so this is the
step that makes the web UI reachable from another machine.

Anything that is not a loopback address requires a token, generated on
first start into /etc/open-linux-router/api-token. The UI asks for it on
first use.

Examples:
  olr listen 0.0.0.0:8080   reachable from your network
  olr listen 127.0.0.1:8080 this box only, for an ssh tunnel
  olr listen --off          stop listening on the network

Usage:
  olr listen <address> [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for listen
      --off    stop listening on the network

Global Flags:
      --dry-run         show what would change and exit without applying it
  -o, --output string   output format: text|json (default "text")
      --socket string   olrd control socket (default "/run/olr/olrd.sock")
